I am not 100% sure, but I think we could return the reason for failing here too.
e.g. 
```
if res.Failed() {
	errMsg := fmt.Sprintf("transaction reverted (%x)", result.Err)
	if len(res.Revert()) > 0 {
		errMsg += fmt.Sprintf(" (0x%x)", result.Revert())
	}
	return nil, errors.New(errMsg)
}

@rjl493456442 This currently produces the following out put for me:
failed to estimate gas needed: always failing transaction (execution reverted) (0x08c379a...000)
Which means users would have to parse the error string by hand whenever they want to check the reason, maybe we could add an error type with an attribute "reason" or something.

I would propose an error type like this 
```
type failedTxError struct {
error_type string
reason string
}
```
So user would not have to manually parse the "always failing tx..." string.

Additionally I think we could decode the reason, as reasons are encoded as if they were a call to `Error(String)` see https://solidity.readthedocs.io/en/latest/control-structures.html#revert

Decoding would become: 
```
if bytes.Equal(rval[:4], crypto.Keccak256([]byte("Error(string)"))[:4]) {
		str, _ := abi.NewType("string", "", nil)
		errorDesc, err := abi.Arguments{{Type: str}}.UnpackValues(rval[4:])
		if err != nil {
			return nil, err
		}
		panic(errorDesc[0].(string))
		return nil, errors.New(errorDesc[0].(string))
	}
```
